  "text": " for research and that was later applied. Now why do you, if I can sense your point, what's for it? Why do you think right after World War II, this would have been a concern? Well, it was obviously a response to the doctors trial at Nuremberg, where the doctors who- Of Nazi doctors. Nazi doctors who had conducted medical research in the death camps, in the concentration camps, on prisoners and had tried to justify it on various grounds. And actually they would say things like, well, the prisoners were treated better on the medical wards than they were in the general camp. They were gonna die anyways. And so actually giving them better food and better housing and better care before they died was an act of kindness. And this was serving the greater good, serving the greater good view. Exactly. And Nazi medicine had taken a subtle shift from treating the individual vulnerable patient in front of me to being in the service of the health of the Volk, the social organism as a whole. Well, if the social organism as a whole is where my loyalties lies of physician, what happens if the social organism has a quote unquote cancer on it? If there's the sick, the disabled, the people who are a drain on the economy, well, you carve out the cancer and you do away with it. And that led to the euthanasia regime. That led to 600,000 people involuntarily sterilized by the Nazi doctors, even before the Nazis came to power. This was German medicine, the best most advanced medical system and medical institutions in the world went down this path because they began to see their job as rather than the traditional Hippocratic ethic, my loyalty is to the sick patient who deserves the best care possible. No, my loyalty is to, again, the social organism as a whole. Well, when the state or the social program becomes disordered and misdirected, then the whole medical profession starts following in that wrong direction. And that's what happened to German medicine in the 19, starting in the 1920s actually, Tucker, but on into the 1930s and through the Second World War.",
